## Suites that can fail under load and pass alone

These suites have failed inside a shared full run and passed in isolation.
That does not establish the cause or erase the failed run. An isolated rerun
is diagnostic evidence; record the process, server or temporary-state
mechanism before accepting a correction or replacing a full-run result.

- `tests/card/actions/atomic-saves.test.ts` — its first-use engine build shares
  temporary state with concurrent suites.
- `tests/exercise/accounts-3.test.ts`,
  `tests/card/auth/first-run-setup-endpoint.test.ts` and
  the reset-flow endpoint suite (not in the public package) — each boots a local server
  and races the shared temporary directory during auth setup. (On
  2026-10-14 the reset-flow suite failed one check inside a full run and
  passed all 14 alone.)
- `tests/tools/shadow/migration-parity-shadow.test.mjs` — drives a stub
  instrument that spawns processes; under contention a varying subset of its 68
  tests fails, a different subset each time.
- `tests/server/blob-bunny-mock.test.ts` — runs a mock storage server on a
  loopback port, which can lose the port under load (`fetch failed`).

## The MySQL lane: the port's id allocation diverges from Ruby's

The recorded 2026-10-15 MySQL 8.4.11 runs use the pinned Ruby 3.3.12
oracle. `tools/same-database/mysql-lane-compare.mjs` compares rendering and
semantics only: Ruby and the port agree on 1752 of 1752 render and 2388 of
2388 semantics operations. It does not establish scenario parity.
The lane overlay `fixtures/synthetic-v1/expected-lanes/mysql/` holds 6
render, 3 semantics and 11 scenario answers copied byte-for-byte from the
recorded stock Ruby-on-MySQL run. These are Ruby's lane expectations,
including the `Not Topics` ties and the scenario writes' ids. Grading the
recorded port run against those expectations fails:
rendering 1754 of 1754, semantics 2389 of 2389, but persistence 4 of 16 and
side effects 15 of 19. The failures are the write steps' card ids, the same
card created on the same database with a different id: `create-tag-as-member`
Ruby 916, port 911; `create-memo-member-then-read-denied-anonymous` Ruby 915,
port 911; `create-compound-on-new-left` Ruby 919, port 913; and the rows that
name those ids. The mechanism is not yet established: Ruby's ids come from
MySQL's auto-increment counter, which InnoDB advances on a rolled-back insert,
while the port computes the next id from the current rows
(`src/card/history/write-path.ts`). The finding is left open, not declared and
not overlaid.

## The signed-in edit-link parity regression: fixed at this tree, and how to tell

This ledger used to carry a row for
`tests/card/mod-loader/card-mod-edit-views.test.ts`, and that row carried the
only written account of the signed-in editing parity regression. The row was
removed when the ledger was regenerated from a full run of this tree (commit
39b46ef3, 2026-10-14), because the suite passes. A deleted row cannot say
whether a defect was fixed or forgotten, so this section says which it was.

**The defect.** The guide rule card's edit link was rendered for every
viewer. `guide.rb` prepends the link only when the guide card is allowed to
be updated. The port's gate could only deny: it asked the engine for that
card's update verdict and suppressed the link only on an explicit refusal.
A verdict the engine had not established was not a refusal, the guide rule
card is nested rather than a render root, so its verdict was not established
for it, and the link rendered for viewers who must not see it.

**The fix.** The gate now reads the task seam's deny-by-default posture. Both
ports of the helper ask for the verdict and render the link only when the
answer is an affirmative `true`:

- `src/card/mod-loader/mods/card-mod-edit.ts:2308`
- `src/card/mod-loader/mods/card-mod-help.ts:290`

Before the fix both read
`if (taskAllowed !== undefined && taskAllowed === false) return text;`. The
fix landed in commit 17ce204a, 2026-10-11.

**The evidence, at this tree.** The Ruby recording is committed
(`fixtures/captured/edit-views.json`). It carries one `edit-link` in the guide
surfaces for the always-ok stand-in and none for the plain-member stand-in and
none for the anonymous stand-in. The suite that pins those bytes renders the
same three arms and compares them byte for byte, and it is green here:
`tests/card/mod-loader/card-mod-edit-views.test.ts`, 14 tests, passing in the
full correctness run of 2026-10-25 on this tree (commit `c17906db`).

So the regression is fixed, and the fix is the port rendering the bytes the
Ruby recording already held. A reader who wants to check it does not have to
take this section's word for it: the recording and the suite are both in the
tree.
